processCampaign records internalSales on every path except reuse_existing
(both the dry-run and the live record() call) and skip_host_offline. On those
rows the field was the schema default on a fresh row, or whatever an earlier
decision on the same (game, campaignId) row left there — which is how 15 Black
Desert reuse rows came to say internalSales 0 while SaleSignal held 13 sales
(FARM2-VERIFICATION §5.1). One field added to three record() calls; the value
is the `internalSales` already computed at the top of the function from the
same salesOf() the sellability gate used. Own commit so it can be held.

What this deliberately does NOT do: widen the replay harness's write-map
(decisionClasses.OMITS_INTERNAL_SALES). That map gates an integrity cross-check
against the flat field, and every row written before this commit still carries
the old value on these two paths. Widening it would turn the 15 Black Desert
rows from `agree` back into `sales_count_mismatch`. Whether the flat field can
be trusted is a property of WHEN the row was written, which the harness cannot
see; rows written since the decisionInputs snapshot shipped never consult the
flat field at all, so nothing is lost by leaving the map alone. A test pins
that it stays.

Four tests drive the real processCampaign: the host gate now records the sales
the gate saw; the dry-run reuse record does too (the Black Desert path itself,
reached via ctx.reusableMap and ctx.hostState.hasFile, no host needed); a stale
leftover from an earlier decision is overwritten on re-decision; and the
write-map is unchanged. The LIVE reuse record() call restarts containers and
cannot be reached from a sandbox — it carries the identical one-field change
and is covered by inspection only.

Suite 698/699 vs 694/695 after the previous commit; the single failure is
unchanged.
